  "summary": "A pressure sustaining (back-pressure) valve keeps the pressure UPSTREAM of it from falling below a set value. A small adjustable pilot senses inlet pressure: when inlet pressure rises above the set point the pilot vents the control chamber and the main valve opens further; when it falls, the pilot lets inlet pressure into the chamber and the main valve throttles. It protects high zones of water networks, prevents pump run-out and maintains back-pressure on processes.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Pilot-operated automatic control valve",
    "type": "Hydraulically operated diaphragm globe valve with sustaining pilot"
  },
